#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162744} :activity/id #uuid "2faf287c-6117-590f-9c73-9dc1ca650d9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050266 :topic/id #uuid "3af6b95a-292c-5d96-81be-58bbf073174a" :topic/math-academy-id 3023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163177 :tutorial/content "**The chi-square distribution** with $k$ **degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi^2(k)$, is the distribution of a random variable that is the sum of the squares of $k$ independent standard normal random variables. \n\nThus, if $Z_1, Z_2, \\ldots, Z_k$ are mutually independent, where $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$, then\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\ldots+Z_k^2\\sim  \\chi^2(k).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $X \\sim \\chi^2(k)$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a46942ee5d42c721fb23905e49a67d28c2916eecc234e86734263c599df49fc.svg)\n\nNote that $\\Gamma$ is the gamma function.\n\nThe diagram below shows $f(x)$ for various degrees of freedom $k.$\n\n![](images/ce/cecc3fc82e618f5b5f91e61c4dcab6c1d38b970af20f0ae2baf929b0ce9cdbb3.png)\n\nThe chi-square distribution is widely used in inferential statistics. In particular, it is used in **goodness of fit** tests, where experimental data is compared against a hypothesized theoretical model. You will learn more about the applications of the chi-square distribution in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "8adfa155-2603-5b6f-9206-c73523985df0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10602 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2249706e-9b5b-5da2-bc3a-0a2a5588a700" :step/math-academy-id 217601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161732 :question/id #uuid "531bdbe2-35a5-599f-8009-f65888b6f05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80bc6ce-5b86-5883-a2bb-dbdc1e5503de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434098}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability for a Chi-Square Random Variable Using the PDF"} :step/id #uuid "352f1f5d-a28b-5167-a9a4-4b4ead2412ae" :step/math-academy-id 217602 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163178 :tutorial/content "Let $X\\sim\\chi^2(3).$ Suppose we wish to know the specific value $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = 0.05.\n$$\n\nHere, $\\alpha = 0.05$ is sometimes called the **significance level**. The required probability is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/34/34551dd57f17cfb0bdd3bebd2d09c907615f3d936e4b5d69083517d17c1f779f.png)\n\nWe often resort to percentage points tables to look up our desired values rather than computing these probabilities by hand (which would be very difficult). \n\nSome percentage points for the $\\chi^2(3)$ distribution are given below. Each cell in the table gives a value $x$ such that $P(X\\geq x) = p$ for a particular value of $p.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,9.348\\,$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,12.838\\,$ |\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 7.815) = 0.05.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 7.815.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fad9ff3a-2abf-529e-b457-a9b2a3922621" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10603 :tutorial/title "Working With a Percentage Points Table"} :step/id #uuid "2f6a8301-e0ac-56f8-b16c-4cfeffffbf6b" :step/math-academy-id 217605 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161733 :question/id #uuid "1c328fe8-ba46-51f0-acb3-db3781a9d859" :question/math-academy-id "e-16429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492920}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"} :step/id #uuid "84f7b979-7b8d-5bfd-8f98-e8e476f25db4" :step/math-academy-id 217606 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161734 :question/id #uuid "42104104-5483-5c90-be3f-dbe7b10cbff3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492908}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "eded01af-a1e8-5d72-b095-7ea6b9995e46" :step/math-academy-id 217609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :question/id #uuid "ab5d8857-fdb8-5f4f-85cc-ba9d9e480081" :question/math-academy-id "e-16432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492862}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d2184a6e-f98c-5e40-9508-232f506261eb" :step/math-academy-id 217613}] :activity/title "The Chi-Square Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162177}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492933} :activity/id #uuid "f53cd9ea-6553-5efb-a7b8-f3c13f72a44b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050266 :topic/id #uuid "3af6b95a-292c-5d96-81be-58bbf073174a" :topic/math-academy-id 3023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35116ec5-d52d-513b-98d1-ee2dc4c2b818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.237" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492328} {:answer/id #uuid "5a842dfb-5f31-5980-9368-0282ea1061e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492395} {:answer/id #uuid "72d1ed37-b2d0-5a6f-8edc-f8327c9400ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492445} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6daf69-0ff6-5b40-87e3-4c42a1bf0569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.635" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492594} {:answer/id #uuid "d8770aef-7d05-5470-a900-6fc714024af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fb6871-9764-57d3-a93a-ed370615662e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9a27da7c-7cd5-581c-8018-40a7c4e9ff5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108258" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(6),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.9.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(6),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=6.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>2.204)=0.900.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.204.$"} :step/id #uuid "14676990-70ab-5297-b2c1-9c3014d28e45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b9b65f2-32b1-5306-a674-1a8cce394049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.711" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492344} {:answer/id #uuid "487a0cb4-3b3c-53b1-ad75-19be1635b445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492362} {:answer/id #uuid "760efcf4-e371-5ee5-8095-2afc058abad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492455} {:answer/id #uuid "801fb805-5abf-578a-bed5-8eed93de26a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492473} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fc4ad5-887a-55cb-91ae-8042270e76d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492640} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb2367b-54fc-50aa-8684-3aa2a710859d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1169ae0-dbcc-5afc-b27c-5d1844b9830b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108271" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.005.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,0.072\\,$ | $\\,0.115\\,$ | $\\,0.216\\,$ | $\\,0.352\\,$ | $\\,0.584\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.005 \\\\  & =0.995\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=4.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>0.207)=0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.207.$"} :step/id #uuid "8743147f-7556-5e5e-a7d0-2c7a8c3a230d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5731441f-975d-55af-aa2c-666c2201d3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60.346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492382} :answer-field/id #uuid "346fec95-da88-5e3b-8992-afee68fefadc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c65221c-bb24-5314-9566-2deac5724c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-252041" :question/problem "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{50}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 50.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\le y)=0.85.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $48$ | $\\,73.683\\,$ | $\\,65.171\\,$ | $\\,60.907\\,$ | $\\,58.135\\,$ | $\\,55.993\\,$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,74.919\\,$ | $\\,66.339\\,$ | $\\,62.038\\,$ | $\\,59.241\\,$ | $\\,57.079\\,$ |\n| $50$ | $\\,76.154\\,$ | $\\,67.505\\,$ | $\\,63.167\\,$ | $\\,60.346\\,$ | $\\,58.164\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\le y)=0.85$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{50}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(50).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\ge y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.85 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $48$ | $\\,73.683\\,$ | $\\,65.171\\,$ | $\\,60.907\\,$ | $\\,58.135\\,$ | $\\,55.993\\,$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,74.919\\,$ | $\\,66.339\\,$ | $\\,62.038\\,$ | $\\,59.241\\,$ | $\\,57.079\\,$ |\n| $50$ | $\\,76.154\\,$ | $\\,67.505\\,$ | $\\,63.167\\,$ | $\\,60.346\\,$ | $\\,58.164\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 60.346)=0.15\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 60.346)=0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=60.346.$"} :step/id #uuid "d35e5081-77a0-59a9-80ae-7f654b2e8813"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a003eec-c940-5c52-8afb-17ca2497bd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492492} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6dfc5f-3b5e-5c28-bb1f-1e1d33b68efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.690" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492518} {:answer/id #uuid "a204a67c-2aac-5f78-851c-80a0a1dad1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.646" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492543} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd4c622-93dc-5665-8894-49abfb499799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492649} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ee4dbe-2f70-5ddb-a8de-19cafb15b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.989" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492518} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb6b39f-0033-59e4-bf9a-66aa3b7e8c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed8bb07d-ed88-5d7c-a376-b5b20e72fc61" :question/math-academy-id "q-108263" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(7),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.975.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>1.690)=0.975.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.690.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd8b0eaf-0121-540c-b3dc-4528fcf15e64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b19f2af-663c-568d-918b-fdd147dba0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492342} {:answer/id #uuid "b7445500-61aa-5ddc-98e2-42d724529aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492587} {:answer/id #uuid "c56686cf-7c83-57b3-ba6f-2e96ffe3b304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492612} {:answer/id #uuid "d9282e28-6b69-59ec-8d69-1f20cf128cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.711" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492644} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7e3563-001d-5b37-b49f-11776d7b682b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492342} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f51432e-ecd0-5626-b4b9-dbd209747e43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4dc7492-4019-5d98-a361-736ee5b48968" :question/math-academy-id "q-108259" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.995.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | -